data.content:
## What breaks in .NET 11

In .NET 11, the **sync-over-async opt-in for the EF Core Cosmos DB provider is removed entirely**. The configuration you used in .NET 10 (e.g., `AllowSyncOverAsync()` on the Cosmos options) no longer compiles or is silently ignored.

All synchronous LINQ operators — `ToList()`, `First()`, `Count()`, and `SaveChanges()` — will throw **`InvalidOperationException`** at runtime because the Cosmos provider has no true synchronous I/O path.

## Fix: convert to async

```csharp
public class ProductRepository
{
    private readonly CosmosDbContext _context;

    public async Task<List<Product>> GetAllAsync()
    {
        return await _context.Products.ToListAsync();
    }

    public async Task<Product> GetByIdAsync(int id)
    {
        return await _context.Products.FirstAsync(p => p.Id == id);
    }

    public async Task<int> GetCountAsync()
    {
        return await _context.Products.CountAsync();
    }

    public async Task SaveAsync(Product product)
    {
        _context.Products.Add(product);
        await _context.SaveChangesAsync();
    }
}
```

**Summary of the progression:**

| Version | Behavior |
|---------|----------|
| .NET 9 | Sync throws by default; opt-in added |
| .NET 10 | Opt-in still works but marked `[Obsolete]` |
| .NET 11 | Opt-in removed; sync always throws |

You'll need to propagate `async`/`await` up through your call stack (controllers, services, etc.) wherever these repository methods are called.
